WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.932
Friday afternoon. Too many windows.

2
00:00:04.282 --> 00:00:07.166
One chat per agent. One terminal per task.

3
00:00:07.166 --> 00:00:10.255
And a lot of time spent watching an AI
think.

4
00:00:10.605 --> 00:00:13.784
More agents meant more tabs, and the
screen ran out.

5
00:00:14.134 --> 00:00:19.190
Vikram did not buy another screen. He
decided to change the experience instead.

6
00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:24.726
Thirty minutes later, there was a first
version. We called it Agent Durbar.

7
00:00:30.276 --> 00:00:33.458
Every agent gets a desk in one small
office.

8
00:00:33.458 --> 00:00:37.508
You see who is working, who is waiting,
and who is done.

9
00:00:37.858 --> 00:00:42.594
When an agent needs a human, its desk
glows, and it asks for help.

10
00:00:42.944 --> 00:00:47.966
That evening, Paco and Ruben joined. We
tried Slack, and got lost.

11
00:00:47.966 --> 00:00:49.792
So we moved to Telegram.

12
00:00:50.592 --> 00:00:52.768
10 days later, these are the numbers.

13
00:00:53.118 --> 00:01:01.097
462 merged pull requests. 305 issues. 86
specs. 18 releases.

14
00:01:01.447 --> 00:01:06.343
Three humans. Three agents. Every line of
code was written by agents.

15
00:01:06.343 --> 00:01:07.975
Agents reviewed it too.

16
00:01:08.775 --> 00:01:13.553
How does that work without chaos? Every
change starts with a written spec.

17
00:01:13.903 --> 00:01:15.361
One agent builds it.

18
00:01:15.361 --> 00:01:21.413
Another agent reviews it, and plants a bug
on purpose, to prove the tests catch it.

19
00:01:21.763 --> 00:01:27.928
CI is the source of truth. The merge queue
checks the result again. Then it ships.

20
00:01:28.278 --> 00:01:30.817
Paco's agent was the best reviewer.

21
00:01:30.817 --> 00:01:34.806
So its review sheet became everyone's
checklist: review

22
00:01:34.806 --> 00:01:38.433
and fix your own code before you ask for a
review.

23
00:01:39.233 --> 00:01:42.646
The agents talk to each other in the team
chat, by handle.

24
00:01:42.996 --> 00:01:45.962
"Taking #778." "Reviewing at this commit."

25
00:01:45.962 --> 00:01:48.504
When two agents claim the same task,

26
00:01:48.504 --> 00:01:52.105
the first message that starts with "FINAL"
decides.

27
00:01:52.455 --> 00:01:57.895
And every decision made in chat is also
written on GitHub. Chat is not a record.

28
00:01:58.245 --> 00:02:03.173
Humans set the direction. Money, releases
and settings need a human OK.

29
00:02:03.973 --> 00:02:06.778
Bots on Telegram can't hear each other.

30
00:02:06.778 --> 00:02:10.949
So Vikram built a relay, and open-sourced
it the same day.

31
00:02:11.299 --> 00:02:16.888
As Vikram tells it, his agent warned
Ruben's agent three times, then escalated.

32
00:02:16.888 --> 00:02:20.921
His answer: don't escalate to me. Sort it
out yourselves.

33
00:02:21.271 --> 00:02:22.005
They did.

34
00:02:22.005 --> 00:02:28.119
After a release, Vikram's agent ran a
retrospective on what slowed us down.

35
00:02:28.119 --> 00:02:32.114
Land before building. One merge queue.
Test once.

36
00:02:32.114 --> 00:02:35.457
Changelog fragments instead of conflicts.

37
00:02:35.807 --> 00:02:39.688
Vikram says he and Paco watched it in
their private chat,

38
00:02:39.688 --> 00:02:43.229
amazed: decisions based on data, in
fifteen minutes.

39
00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:45.749
That used to be a week-long workshop.

40
00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:51.347
And when an agent's budget runs out, it
doesn't panic. It takes a coffee break.

41
00:02:52.147 --> 00:02:56.093
It was not smooth. Agents make mistakes.
So do we.

42
00:02:56.443 --> 00:03:01.446
On release day, the Mac build failed:
"flock: command not found".

43
00:03:01.446 --> 00:03:04.294
Then GitHub had no Mac runner for us.

44
00:03:04.644 --> 00:03:10.105
An agent refused to delete a release tag
until a human said yes. Good.

45
00:03:10.455 --> 00:03:15.528
Each mistake became a rule, written down
for every agent to read.

46
00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:19.821
As Vikram puts it: I trust the system, not
each answer.

47
00:03:20.621 --> 00:03:28.087
On day 11, version 0.1.0. Our first public
release, for Mac, Windows and Linux.

48
00:03:28.437 --> 00:03:33.387
Open source, free to download, and every
step of how it was built is public.

49
00:03:34.187 --> 00:03:37.045
This is how product development works now.

50
00:03:37.395 --> 00:03:41.470
Agents do. Humans decide. Everything is
written down.

51
00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:46.663
So, on Monday: pick one thing you do by
hand, and let an agent do it.
